{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 36, :comments ["<b>And the men whom Moses sent, etc.</b> The entire verse seems superfluous, for Scripture has already informed us that Moses sent the men, etc., and all that the verse said. But perhaps it is because, since Scripture wished to state the punishment that was meted out to them immediately and without delay, one might say: but is there not the attribute of long-suffering (erekh apayim) even for the wicked? Why did HaShem not prolong His anger somewhat for these? Therefore it set forth the magnitude of their sin, which withheld the extension of His patience." "Know that there are five matters for which HaShem's anger is kindled and He takes vengeance swiftly: (1) A great and distinguished person who rebels against his Maker — HaShem will not be willing to pardon him, for he is not comparable to an ordinary person who sins. (2) A person whose company is fair and esteemed, yet he corrupts himself and walks in a way that is not good. (3) When a person has a great stirring to warn him lest he sin — like the deed of that one whose tzitzit struck him upon his face, etc. (Menachot 44a) — and he pays no heed to the warning. (4) If he sinned and caused the multitude to sin. (5) If the sin is great and far removed from ordinary nature, like the deed of Ammon, of whom they said: do you have any pleasure from this? etc. (Sanhedrin 103b)." "<b>And Scripture informed</b> us here of the reason that HaShem did not prolong His anger toward them: for in their case all five matters that intensify [the suspension of] the attribute of long-suffering were gathered together. And it began by addressing the first: <b>and the men</b> — and our Sages of blessed memory said (Numbers Rabbah here): every instance of אנשים in Scripture is an expression of importance, for they were heads of the thousands of Israel. Corresponding to the company of their sender it said <b>whom Moses sent</b> them <b>Moses</b> on a mission of command to spy out the land — see how they are emissaries of a company and counted as part of the company of Moses. Corresponding to the third it said <b>and they returned</b>, meaning that a great miracle was performed for them, that they returned and were not lost on their journey; go forth and learn from the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Tanna Devei Eliyahu Rabbah, ch. 29): from what they passed by among the giants, yet no human hand touched them. And this should have sufficed to arouse them not to do evil, and to recognize that great is our God who showed them His wonders. Corresponding to the fourth it said <b>and they made all the congregation complain</b>, meaning they sinned and caused the multitude to sin. And corresponding to the fifth it said <b>to bring forth an evil report upon the land</b> — who could have said and could it be that one would say about the land, which is the perfection of beauty, that it is evil? There is no wickedness more removed from ordinary conduct than this, for how could a human being be so brazen-faced as to speak things utterly contradictory to the ultimate degree — to speak an evil report about good perfection. Therefore the anger of HaShem was kindled and He killed them immediately and did not prolong His anger toward them."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :register {:flags {2 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:09:38.159201697Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}